<h1>Lempel-Ziv-Welch</h1>
<p>
(algorithm)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A compression algorithm that codes strings of characters with codes of a fixed number of bits.  Every new string in the input is added to a table until it is full.  The codes of existing strings are output instead of the strings.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Also known as</strong> LZW compression.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="algorithmBSTW.html"><em>algorithm BSTW</em></a>.
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorGS">GS</a></p>
<h2>Implementation</h2>
Mark Nelson's article with an <a href="https://marknelson.us/posts/2011/11/08/lzw-revisited.html" target="_blank">explanation (C++)</a>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
<a href="http://www.ics.uci.edu/~dan/pubs/DC-Sec5.html" target="_blank">survey of data compression</a>
</p>
<p>
<strong>J. Ziv and A. Lempel</strong>, <em>A Universal Algorithm for Sequential Data Compression</em>, IEEE Transactions on Information Theory, May 1977. </p> <p> <strong>Terry Welch</strong>, <em>A Technique for High-Performance Data Compression</em>, Computer, June 1984.
</p>

